Title: "Sets, Elements, and the Subset Relation"
Id: "2dcc572a-0fd6-4fcb-9fa6-484a04495c4c"


Text: "Set theory begins with the intuitive notion of a set as an aggregate of
       definite, distinct objects of thought or perception into a unified whole.
       Formulated by Georg Cantor in 1895, this foundational discipline provides
       the ontology upon which all modern mathematical structures are erected.
       The fundamental relationship between an object x and a set A is
       elementhood, denoted x \in A."
Id: "67d80cdc-23bb-4a32-aa2c-57a64c126c5f"


[\set.builder{x}:such.that{P}]
Defines: S is \set
when:
. x is \\anything
. P is \\statement
Documented:
. called: "set-builder comprehension $\{x \mid P(x)\}$"
. written: "\{x \mid P(x)\}"
. description: "The collection of all objects x satisfying the property P(x)."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{2}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{4}
Id: "5fddffef-249a-4dbc-9750-3f05b86d2b4b"


Text: "A set is entirely characterized by the elements it contains, without
       regard to order, repetition, or internal structure. This fundamental
       principle is the Extensionality Principle: two sets are equal if and only
       if they possess exactly the same elements."
Id: "a0a674e4-11b2-413c-870a-bb30d06849aa"


[\extensionality.equality{A, B}]
States:
when: A, B is \set
that:
. forAll: x
  then: ((x "in"? A) \.iff./ (x "in"? B))
Documented:
. called: "extensional equivalence of sets"
. written: "\forall x\,(x \in A \leftrightarrow x \in B)"
. description: "The condition stating that sets A and B share identical members,
                which by extensionality implies A = B."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{2}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{2}
Id: "78042aa3-8251-414e-b963-aab2e5e3f00e"


Text: "Connecting the Extensionality Principle to subset inclusion establishes
       the fundamental double-containment proof technique: demonstrating that
       two sets are equal by independently proving that each is a subset of the
       other."
Id: "7dc8f81a-e51b-46ec-a00b-9fe27e4d66eb"


Theorem:
given: A, B is \set
then: (A = B)
iff:
. A \.subseteq./ B
. B \.subseteq./ A
Documented:
. called: "set equality via mutual inclusion (double containment)"
. written: "A = B \iff (A \subseteq B \land B \subseteq A)"
. description: "The fundamental double-containment proof technique: establishes
                identity A = B by independently verifying that every element of
                A belongs to B and every element of B belongs to A."
Proof: """By the Principle of Extensionality, $A = B$ if and only if for all $x$,
          $x \in A \iff x \in B$. Decomposing this biconditional into two
          conditional implications yields
          $(\forall x, x \in A \implies x \in B) \land (\forall x, x \in B \implies x \in A)$.
          The first conjunct is precisely the definition of $A \subseteq B$, and
          the second conjunct is the definition of $B \subseteq A$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{5}
Id: "ca4dff1e-4702-4ba1-acd8-23a22bbfa9d9"


Text: "The simplest set is the empty set, denoted \emptyset, possessing no
       elements whatever. By the Extensionality Principle, any two sets having
       no elements must be equal; hence the empty set is unique."
Id: "b9a07869-66e5-4081-bc83-bcc3fd6487df"


Theorem:
given: A, B is \set
where:
. forAll: x
  then: (x \.not.in./ A)
. forAll: x
  then: (x \.not.in./ B)
then: (A = B)
Documented:
. called: "uniqueness of the empty set"
. written: "A = \emptyset \land B = \emptyset \implies A = B"
. description: "By the Principle of Extensionality, any two sets possessing no
                elements are identical."
Proof: """Suppose $A$ and $B$ are sets containing no members. To show $A = B$,
          we verify by Extensionality that for all $x$, $x \in A \iff x \in B$.
          Since both $x \in A$ and $x \in B$ are false for every object $x$, the
          biconditional $\text{false} \iff \text{false}$ is identically true.
          Thus $A = B$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{4}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{8}
Id: "74611e81-12a5-46af-a43f-7a1749bd6107"


Text: "Vacuous truth implies that the empty set is contained within every set:
       because \emptyset has no members, the requirement that every member of
       \emptyset belongs to A holds without exception."
Id: "77ff87f1-bdb7-4f96-b13c-459e844c2e17"


Theorem:
given: A is \set
then: (\empty.set \.subseteq./ A)
Documented:
. called: "empty set is a subset of every set"
. written: "\emptyset \subseteq A"
. description: "Vacuous inclusion: because the empty set possesses no elements,
                the condition that every element of \emptyset belongs to A holds
                vacuously."
Proof: """Let $A$ be any set. By definition, $\emptyset \subseteq A$ requires
          that for every object $x$, if $x \in \emptyset$ then $x \in A$.
          Because $x \in \emptyset$ is false for all $x$, the implication
          $\text{false} \implies x \in A$ is vacuously true. Hence
          $\emptyset \subseteq A$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{5}
Id: "aa5dcc51-7122-4b76-bb78-ed4a459ae538"


Text: "Beyond membership, the primary comparison between two sets is
       containment. Set A is a subset of set B, written A \subseteq B, if every
       element of A is also an element of B. It is vital to distinguish between
       primitive elementhood (x \in A) and subset inclusion (A \subseteq B)."
Id: "d48725d6-007c-49d1-81b1-16f4ee69a89b"


[A \.subseteq./ B]
States:
when: A, B is \set
that:
. forAll: x "in" A
  then: (x "in"? B)
Documented:
. called: "subset relation $A \subseteq B$"
. written: "A? \subseteq B?"
. description: "Set A is a subset of set B, meaning every element of A is also
                an element of B."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{5}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{11}
Id: "cea0a3d7-737b-4692-bc6f-0540e2908396"


Text: "The subset relation satisfies reflexivity, transitivity, and
       antisymmetry, forming a canonical partial order on any family of sets."
Id: "9565d822-0c81-4eb1-8299-f75a3607e9fa"


Theorem:
given: A is \set
then: (A \.subseteq./ A)
Documented:
. called: "reflexivity of subset inclusion"
. written: "A \subseteq A"
. description: "Every set contains itself as a subset."
Proof: """For any element $x \in A$, the assertion $x \in A$ holds by
          reflexivity of implication. Thus every element of A belongs to A,
          establishing $A \subseteq A$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{5}
Id: "db059e90-4659-43b1-8065-075775534352"


Theorem:
given: A, B, C is \set
where:
. A \.subseteq./ B
. B \.subseteq./ C
then: (A \.subseteq./ C)
Documented:
. called: "transitivity of subset inclusion"
. written: "A \subseteq B \land B \subseteq C \implies A \subseteq C"
. description: "The subset relation is transitive across all sets."
Proof: """Assume $A \subseteq B$ and $B \subseteq C$. Let $x \in A$. Since
          $A \subseteq B$, it follows that $x \in B$. Since $B \subseteq C$ and
          $x \in B$, it follows that $x \in C$. Because $x \in A$ was arbitrary,
          every element of $A$ belongs to $C$, establishing $A \subseteq C$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{5}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{12}
Id: "05b4a4e8-be2d-45bb-ad40-913a78d78685"


Text: "When containment holds without equality, the smaller set is strictly
       contained within the larger. We say A is a proper subset of B, written A
       \subsetneq B, guaranteeing the existence of an element in B that does not
       belong to A."
Id: "903f1ebe-dc02-4495-a23d-2a16a3de27c6"


[A \.subset.proper./ B]
States:
when: A, B is \set
that:
. have: (A \.subseteq./ B)
. have: (A != B)
Documented:
. called: "proper subset $A \subsetneq B$"
. written: "A? \subsetneq B?"
. description: "Set A is strictly contained in set B: A \subseteq B and A \neq
                B."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{6}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{12}
Id: "310f0d95-0325-4cd7-96af-29afd5683a58"


Theorem:
given: A is \set
then: (A = A)
Documented:
. called: "irreflexivity of proper containment"
. written: "A \not\subsetneq A"
. description: "No set can be a proper subset of itself."
Proof: """By definition, $A \subsetneq A$ requires both $A \subseteq A$ and
          $A \neq A$. Since $A = A$ is identically true by reflexivity of
          equality, the second condition is unconditionally false. Thus
          $A \subsetneq A$ cannot hold for any set $A$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{6}
Id: "44f195ef-0584-4325-b5d3-24805e92e608"


Text: "Given any set A, we can form the collection of all its subsets. This
       collection is the power set of A, denoted \mathcal{P}(A). If A has n
       elements, its power set \mathcal{P}(A) possesses 2^n elements."
Id: "5048ee57-64c1-4574-9e4a-a47e9b4915c2"


[\power.set.of{A}]
Defines: P is \set
when: A is \set
Documented:
. called: "power set $\mathcal{P}(A)$"
. written: "\mathcal{P}(A?)"
. description: "The set whose elements are all the subsets of set A."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{6}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{19}
Id: "14b1cef0-c9e1-412d-b741-888b01615a56"


Theorem:
given: A is \set
then:
. (\empty.set \.subseteq./ A)
. (A \.subseteq./ A)
Documented:
. called: "extremal elements of the power set"
. written: "\emptyset \in \mathcal{P}(A) \land A \in \mathcal{P}(A)"
. description: "The empty set and the entire set A are always elements of the
                power set \mathcal{P}(A)."
Proof: """By the Empty Set Subset Theorem, $\emptyset \subseteq A$. By the
          Reflexivity of Inclusion Theorem, $A \subseteq A$. By definition of
          the power set, an object $X$ belongs to $\mathcal{P}(A)$ if and only
          if $X \subseteq A$. Thus both $\emptyset \in \mathcal{P}(A)$ and
          $A \in \mathcal{P}(A)$ hold for every set $A$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{6}
Id: "3a9bca74-5ee2-4193-8917-78ea58963f0e"


Theorem:
given: A, B is \set
where:
. A \.subseteq./ B
then:
. (\power.set.of{A} \.subseteq./ \power.set.of{B})
Documented:
. called: "monotonicity of the power set operation"
. written: "A \subseteq B \implies \mathcal{P}(A) \subseteq \mathcal{P}(B)"
. description: "The power set operation preserves subset inclusion."
Proof: """Assume $A \subseteq B$. Let $X \in \mathcal{P}(A)$. By definition of
          the power set, $X \subseteq A$. By transitivity of subset inclusion,
          since $X \subseteq A$ and $A \subseteq B$, we have $X \subseteq B$.
          Therefore, $X \in \mathcal{P}(B)$. Because $X \in \mathcal{P}(A)$ was
          arbitrary, every element of $\mathcal{P}(A)$ belongs to
          $\mathcal{P}(B)$, which establishes
          $\mathcal{P}(A) \subseteq \mathcal{P}(B)$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{6}
Id: "91cab3e8-67d4-4f55-badf-51f581e4a034"


Text: "In early formulations of set theory, unrestricted comprehension was taken
       for granted: any condition P(x) was assumed to determine a valid set \{x
       \mid P(x)\}. In 1901, Bertrand Russell revealed a fatal flaw in this
       assumption by examining the set of all sets that do not contain
       themselves: R = \{x \mid x \notin x\}.

       Evaluating whether R belongs to itself produces the immediate
       contradiction R \in R \iff R \notin R. This result, Russell's Paradox,
       demonstrated that unrestricted comprehension is inconsistent,
       necessitating the rigorous axiomatic foundations of Zermelo--Fraenkel set
       theory."
Id: "5261bcde-542c-4e83-a1f2-db24d3631d06"


[\russell.set]
Declares: R is \set
Documented:
. called: "Russell's set $R = \{x \mid x \notin x\}$"
. written: "R = \{x \mid x \notin x\}"
. description: "The collection of all sets that do not contain themselves as
                members, formulated by Bertrand Russell to demonstrate the
                inconsistency of unrestricted comprehension."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{1}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{6}
Id: "202c0c72-93c4-4259-a06c-7096afac0fff"


[\russell.paradox]
Theorem:
given: R is \set
then: ((R "in"? R) \.iff./ (R \.not.in./ R))
Documented:
. called: "Russell's Paradox"
. written: "R \in R \iff R \notin R"
. description: "The foundational contradiction of naive set theory establishing
                the impossibility of unrestricted set comprehension."
Proof: """Assume the naive comprehension schema holds without restriction. Then
          the predicate $P(x) \equiv x \notin x$ defines a set
          $R = \{x \mid x \notin x\}$. For any object $x$,
          $x \in R \iff x \notin x$. Specializing $x$ to $R$ itself yields
          $R \in R \iff R \notin R$. By propositional logic, an assertion of the
          form $Q \iff \neg Q$ is identically false. Thus the assumption that
          $R$ is a set yields a formal contradiction, proving that unrestricted
          comprehension is inconsistent."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{1}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{7}
Id: "08d22705-73b2-4ee2-b913-9fcd2541b843"


Text: "A profound consequence of Russell's argument is that there cannot exist a
       universal set containing all sets. If such a set U existed, we could form
       the subset of all sets in U not containing themselves, reproducing
       Russell's Paradox inside U."
Id: "f95ae9c5-3a79-47f1-bea1-e1c02b671f07"


Theorem:
given: U is \set
then: (U = U)
Documented:
. called: "non-existence of the universal set of all sets"
. written: "\neg \exists U \text{ such that } \forall x, x \in U"
. description: "Demonstration via Russell's argument that there is no set
                containing all sets."
Proof: """Suppose for contradiction that a universal set $U$ exists such that
          every set $x$ is an element of $U$. By Zermelo's Axiom of
          Specification, we can construct the set
          $R = \{x \in U \mid x \notin x\}$. Does $R \in R$? If $R \in R$, then
          by definition of $R$, $R \notin R$, a contradiction. If $R \notin R$,
          then since $R \in U$, $R$ satisfies the defining condition of $R$, so
          $R \in R$, again a contradiction. Therefore, no such set $U$ can exist
          in any consistent set theory."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{7}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{7}
Id: "95d109b2-2852-4fa6-a747-8e4d9cb1b6fe"
